And here has all the information
that Kubernetes knows about it.

231
00:17:35,036 --> 00:17:38,042
It's going to have its IP addresses,
its containers.

232
00:17:38,042 --> 00:17:42,058
Here's some container
information about it.

233
00:17:42,058 --> 00:17:47,351
If we learned about
mounting directories and

234
00:17:47,351 --> 00:17:50,142
various other things.

235
00:17:50,142 --> 00:17:54,184
And here's some event
log [COUGH] about it.

236
00:17:54,184 --> 00:18:02,260
It pulled the image, it successfully
decided where to assign the worker to.

237
00:18:02,260 --> 00:18:05,432
And then it's stated there.

238
00:18:05,432 --> 00:18:10,593
Now if there's any kind of errors with it,
it'll probably show up right there.

239
00:18:10,593 --> 00:18:16,209
All right, so that's that.

240
00:18:16,209 --> 00:18:22,480
I can now say kubectl delete

241
00:18:22,480 --> 00:18:27,393
--force mynginx.

242
00:18:27,393 --> 00:18:31,360
pod mynginx.

243
00:18:31,360 --> 00:18:34,488
All right, and

244
00:18:34,488 --> 00:18:39,541
if I do kubectl get pods,

245
00:18:39,541 --> 00:18:46,769
there's no pods that are running.

246
00:18:46,769 --> 00:18:54,370
All right, so one last, or
let me flip back over to the slides.

247
00:18:55,430 --> 00:19:01,528
Now let's talk about networking, just
enough at this point just to test this.

248
00:19:01,528 --> 00:19:03,656
So when you run a pod,
it gets an IP address.

249
00:19:03,656 --> 00:19:06,410
We saw that IP address.

250
00:19:06,410 --> 00:19:14,578
So that pod, that IP address is
local to the cluster network.

251
00:19:14,578 --> 00:19:19,574
So what the cluster network is,
is it's basically created, Kubernetes

252
00:19:19,574 --> 00:19:25,008
creates its own network where all the pods
can communicate with one another.

253
00:19:25,008 --> 00:19:31,950
But it's outside of all the networking
that is happening on the given workers.

254
00:19:31,950 --> 00:19:38,552
So it's not this publicly
accessible IP address or,

255
00:19:38,552 --> 00:19:45,459
from where I'm running,
which is a host outside of it.

256
00:19:45,459 --> 00:19:53,062
This 1.2.3.5 doesn't have meaning
outside of the Kubernetes cluster.

257
00:19:53,062 --> 00:19:56,238
So the simple mechanism just for

258
00:19:56,238 --> 00:20:00,714
testing is we can do some port-forwarding.

259
00:20:00,714 --> 00:20:05,591
And so this is what allows us to
port-forward from where kubectl

260
00:20:05,591 --> 00:20:08,675
is being run to the Kubernetes network.

261
00:20:08,675 --> 00:20:13,190
So I could say kubectl
port-forward some pod/mynginx.

262
00:20:13,190 --> 00:20:20,296
So I can give it the resource and
then the port mapping I want to see.

263
00:20:20,296 --> 00:20:24,704
All right, so let me rerun kubectl,

264
00:20:24,704 --> 00:20:27,976
apply -f simple-nginx,

265
00:20:27,976 --> 00:20:33,239
get that running again, kubectl get pods,

266
00:20:33,239 --> 00:20:37,664
I can see the container is creating.

267
00:20:37,664 --> 00:20:38,850
It's still creating.

268
00:20:41,270 --> 00:20:47,830
All right, it chose a different worker,
so chances are it's pulling the image.

269
00:20:51,670 --> 00:20:53,912
And there, we got an IP address.

270
00:20:53,912 --> 00:20:56,551
All right, so now it's up and running.

271
00:20:56,551 --> 00:21:02,952
Now let me keep that running and then let
me run the port-forwarding from this one.

272
00:21:02,952 --> 00:21:09,007
So kubectl, port-forward

273
00:21:09,007 --> 00:21:16,583
to pod/mynginx 8000:80.

274
00:21:16,583 --> 00:21:20,860
So internally it'll be port 80,
externally it's be port 8000.

275
00:21:22,030 --> 00:21:24,859
All right, so this goes and

276
00:21:24,859 --> 00:21:30,770
it just won't return until
you cancel out from this.

277
00:21:30,770 --> 00:21:37,094
So what this will allow me
to do is now from outside

278
00:21:37,094 --> 00:21:42,804
of the container, outside of the cluster,

279
00:21:42,804 --> 00:21:49,000
I can do curl 127.0.0.1:8000.

280
00:21:50,890 --> 00:21:57,640
And that's going to handle
a connection for 8000.

281
00:21:58,650 --> 00:22:04,555
So that went through that port-forwarding
to get inside of the cluster and

282
00:22:04,555 --> 00:22:07,097
then communicated with nginx.

283
00:22:07,097 --> 00:22:11,153
And so
then it print out that was the defaults,

284
00:22:11,153 --> 00:22:14,464
what ngInx returns, replies with.

285
00:22:14,464 --> 00:22:18,000
All right, so
that's what port-forwarding does.

286
00:22:19,970 --> 00:22:25,998
The next to know about is controllers.

287
00:22:25,998 --> 00:22:29,908
So we were just creating Pod's directly.

288
00:22:29,908 --> 00:22:37,044
And we could say stuff like kubectl,
apply -f somepod.yaml.

289
00:22:37,044 --> 00:22:42,474
But this is pretty manual and
it's better to not do that,

290
00:22:42,474 --> 00:22:49,617
and instead create another resource
which then creates and manages pods.

291
00:22:49,617 --> 00:22:54,339
So there's something called a deployment.

292
00:22:54,339 --> 00:22:57,602
And so
a deployment is a type of controller, so

293
00:22:57,602 --> 00:23:03,206
there's a bunch of different types of
controllers that exist in Kubernetes.

294
00:23:03,206 --> 00:23:06,564
The deployment is one specific one.

295
00:23:06,564 --> 00:23:10,552
So you describe a desired
state in a Deployment, and

296
00:23:10,552 --> 00:23:15,086
then the Deployment Controller
changes the actual state to

297
00:23:15,086 --> 00:23:18,378
the desired state at a controlled rate.

298
00:23:18,378 --> 00:23:23,708
So it actually controls another
controller (ReplicaSet).

299
00:23:23,708 --> 00:23:29,450
Some uses are we can scale
the number of pods up and down.

300
00:23:29,450 --> 00:23:33,922
We can control or
have a controlled upgrade or downgrade.

301
00:23:33,922 --> 00:23:39,309
So say I have a version one of some
application and then I want to upgrade

302
00:23:39,309 --> 00:23:45,331
it to version two, now I have running six
replicas of it, well, this is a way to

303
00:23:45,331 --> 00:23:50,578
keep the service continuously
running while doing the upgrade.

304
00:23:50,578 --> 00:23:54,060
And maybe I want to do an update
to the pod spec itself.

305
00:23:56,295 --> 00:23:59,927
Alright, so here's the example, YAML.

306
00:23:59,927 --> 00:24:03,445
So again, apiVersion kind,

307
00:24:03,445 --> 00:24:08,238
we're going to say deployment metadata.

308
00:24:08,238 --> 00:24:09,860
We'll talk about that in a second.

309
00:24:09,860 --> 00:24:11,429
And then the spec.

310
00:24:11,429 --> 00:24:12,329
So the spec for

311
00:24:12,329 --> 00:24:16,768
a deployment is a little richer
because it's a little more powerful.

312
00:24:16,768 --> 00:24:19,502
Here the main thing is we're
going to say replicas.

313
00:24:19,502 --> 00:24:22,447
I want it to have three replicas.

314
00:24:22,447 --> 00:24:30,273
And then that specifies how many
pods that are always running.

315
00:24:30,273 --> 00:24:38,336
The next is the selector says which
pod to apply that condition to, right.

316
00:24:38,336 --> 00:24:40,897
And then the template says,

317
00:24:40,897 --> 00:24:45,922
whenever I need to create a new pod,
use this template.

318
00:24:45,922 --> 00:24:51,300
So here's the metadata for that pod
creation and the spec of the pod creation.

319
00:24:51,300 --> 00:24:53,818
So we're going to say the name is nginx.

320
00:24:53,818 --> 00:24:59,464
Images, nginx, right, so the selector.

321
00:24:59,464 --> 00:25:05,422
So when he creates the pod, I think
I have aside on labels and selectors.

322
00:25:05,422 --> 00:25:10,103
So a label is an arbitrary
key-value pair that

323
00:25:10,103 --> 00:25:15,030
you can attach to a resource,
for example a pod.

324
00:25:15,030 --> 00:25:18,557
And then it could be used for
label selection.

325
00:25:18,557 --> 00:25:23,918
So we could do this,
put the label inside the metadata

326
00:25:23,918 --> 00:25:28,340
like that or via kubectl control manually.

327
00:25:28,340 --> 00:25:35,483
So in this picture or in this example of
the deployment, whenever pod is created,

328
00:25:35,483 --> 00:25:41,498
it will create the pod with this label,
the key value pair app nginx.

329
00:25:41,498 --> 00:25:44,900
And it can have multiple labels.

330
00:25:44,900 --> 00:25:50,107
[COUGH] And then for the deployment,
what it uses is this selector.

331
00:25:50,107 --> 00:25:53,972
So it says, here's all the labels
that need to match for

332
00:25:53,972 --> 00:25:58,012
me to consider it as part of
this three replicas app nginx.

333
00:25:58,012 --> 00:26:02,778
Now these should match, but
why it's slightly different is

334
00:26:02,778 --> 00:26:07,824
because let's say I already have,
I've manually created some

335
00:26:07,824 --> 00:26:12,981
pods with these labels and
then I come and run a deployment later.

336
00:26:12,981 --> 00:26:17,572
Well, it will match on
those existing pods and

337
00:26:17,572 --> 00:26:24,174
kind of continually see that it's
kind of part of this deployment.

338
00:26:24,174 --> 00:26:28,561
All right, so that's an aside
on the labels and selectors.

339
00:26:28,561 --> 00:26:31,830
So let's go and try it.

340
00:26:31,830 --> 00:26:35,353
So let's flip back over here.

341
00:26:35,353 --> 00:26:39,682
All right, so
let me stop the port forwarding.

342
00:26:39,682 --> 00:26:45,758
We don't really need that,
kubectl get pods.

343
00:26:45,758 --> 00:26:46,975
I see it's still running.

344
00:26:46,975 --> 00:26:52,351
So I'll say delete force

345
00:26:52,351 --> 00:26:57,220
pod mynginx, great.

346
00:26:57,220 --> 00:26:59,857
So now let's look at the deployment.

347
00:26:59,857 --> 00:27:03,784
Simple nginx deployment.

348
00:27:03,784 --> 00:27:06,235
So here we're going to give it a name.

349
00:27:06,235 --> 00:27:11,019
So this is roughly the same stuff
that was shown in the slide.

350
00:27:11,019 --> 00:27:14,567
And you could see what it looks like.

351
00:27:14,567 --> 00:27:17,636
And you can guess, how do we deploy this?

352
00:27:17,636 --> 00:27:25,936
We say kubectl apply-f
simple nginx-deployment.

353
00:27:25,936 --> 00:27:30,647
And now it's going to get created,
right, so

354
00:27:30,647 --> 00:27:35,621
with that I can, now look,
kubectl get pods.

355
00:27:35,621 --> 00:27:37,960
So you can see I didn't create any pods.

356
00:27:37,960 --> 00:27:39,438
I just created this deployment.

357
00:27:39,438 --> 00:27:42,845
But it said to create four replicas.

358
00:27:42,845 --> 00:27:45,869
And so
you can see it created four replicas.

359
00:27:45,869 --> 00:27:48,858
We can look where they're deployed.

360
00:27:48,858 --> 00:27:55,252
Two of them on worker 2,
two of them on worker 1.

361
00:27:55,252 --> 00:27:58,708
All of them got IP addresses.

362
00:27:58,708 --> 00:28:03,055
If I want to see what deployments I have,

363
00:28:03,055 --> 00:28:07,287
I can say, kubectl get deployments.

364
00:28:07,287 --> 00:28:13,449
And there we can see mynginx
deployment exists and it's running.

365
00:28:13,449 --> 00:28:20,498
As an aside, I can say get all,
which will show everything,

366
00:28:20,498 --> 00:28:28,533
all the different resources that
have been created, okay, right.

367
00:28:28,533 --> 00:28:33,244
So now let's look at if we change

368
00:28:33,244 --> 00:28:37,956
this number of replicas to two.

369
00:28:37,956 --> 00:28:39,194
Let's see what happens.

370
00:28:39,194 --> 00:28:46,340
So let me say kubectl apply -f deployment.

371
00:28:46,340 --> 00:28:50,814
All right, and kubectl get pods.

372
00:28:50,814 --> 00:28:55,178
You can see now only two are running.

373
00:28:55,178 --> 00:28:59,555
If I want to get it again and
change it to four, or

374
00:28:59,555 --> 00:29:03,049
let's change it to three this time.

375
00:29:03,049 --> 00:29:09,815
And so you'll note that these two,
it wasn't like it created two new ones.

376
00:29:09,815 --> 00:29:13,710
That's the exact same one
that was already created.

377
00:29:13,710 --> 00:29:16,909
This one is this one that was created.

378
00:29:16,909 --> 00:29:21,238
And these are the two
that were terminated.

379
00:29:21,238 --> 00:29:24,504
All right, so let me apply it again.

380
00:29:24,504 --> 00:29:30,807
I just increased it to three nodes,
or three pods, three replicas.

381
00:29:30,807 --> 00:29:36,522
And you can see it's now
creating a third one, right.

382
00:29:36,522 --> 00:29:41,637
So to delete,
you can do kubectl delete deployment,

383
00:29:41,637 --> 00:29:47,903
whatever the deployment name is,
which is my nginx deployment.

384
00:29:47,903 --> 00:29:50,343
We're going to leave it running for

385
00:29:50,343 --> 00:29:54,880
now because I want to go into
the next thing real quick, right.

386
00:29:54,880 --> 00:29:59,080
So last concept that we're going to talk
about this time is called a service.

387
00:29:59,080 --> 00:30:04,305
So a service is an abstraction which
defines a logical set of pods and

388
00:30:04,305 --> 00:30:06,884
a policy by which to access them.

389
00:30:06,884 --> 00:30:11,435
So, meaning it load balances between them,
right?

390
00:30:11,435 --> 00:30:15,083
So think of this cloud as a service.

391
00:30:15,083 --> 00:30:17,293
The service is an IP address.

392
00:30:17,293 --> 00:30:19,166
It gets an IP address.

393
00:30:19,166 --> 00:30:26,084
And anytime I want to communicate,
I can say the IP address of the service.

394
00:30:26,084 --> 00:30:32,129
And then it will choose one of
the pods to send that request to.

395
00:30:32,129 --> 00:30:37,468
It will use a selector to see
which nodes should be in that set,

396
00:30:37,468 --> 00:30:41,793
both across scaling and
across failure, right.

397
00:30:41,793 --> 00:30:46,022
So the YAML, let's show that again,

398
00:30:46,022 --> 00:30:50,126
or show that this time for a service.

399
00:30:50,126 --> 00:30:53,228
Again, similar API version kind.

400
00:30:53,228 --> 00:30:56,715
Now we're saying service,
giving it some metadata.

401
00:30:56,715 --> 00:31:02,098
I just want to name the service
because we can reference that.

402
00:31:02,098 --> 00:31:07,033
For port forwarding, we'll see, but
for other things we'll see later and

403
00:31:07,033 --> 00:31:08,045
then the spec.

404
00:31:08,045 --> 00:31:10,662
So the spec here it says,
what is the selector?

405
00:31:10,662 --> 00:31:13,701
That's the main,
most important thing, the selector.

406
00:31:13,701 --> 00:31:16,964
I'm going to say anything
that has app nginx.

407
00:31:16,964 --> 00:31:20,428
Now that should look familiar because
that's what we used in the deployment.

408
00:31:20,428 --> 00:31:27,080
So this should select all of
those pods from that deployment.

409
00:31:27,080 --> 00:31:32,112
And then you can specify other things
like should we do in port translation or

410
00:31:32,112 --> 00:31:33,536
anything like that.

411
00:31:33,536 --> 00:31:36,365
So let's give this a try.

412
00:31:36,365 --> 00:31:43,434
[COUGH] So
you can guess it exists in here.

413
00:31:43,434 --> 00:31:46,397
Simple nginx service.

414
00:31:46,397 --> 00:31:48,088
It's what's shown in the slide.

415
00:31:48,088 --> 00:31:54,540
So I can say kubectl apply
-f simple nginx service.

416
00:31:54,540 --> 00:31:57,770
And it's applied.

417
00:31:57,770 --> 00:32:02,529
I could say kube ctl get all.

418
00:32:02,529 --> 00:32:04,692
If I want to see all the things.

419
00:32:04,692 --> 00:32:05,591
And there we go.

420
00:32:05,591 --> 00:32:11,016
If my service is running,
I could say kubectl get services.

421
00:32:11,016 --> 00:32:14,037
There's my service that's running.

422
00:32:14,037 --> 00:32:18,887
And if I wanted to interact with
it from within the cluster,

423
00:32:18,887 --> 00:32:24,332
I know it has its own IP address,
which is different than the pods.

424
00:32:24,332 --> 00:32:28,413
I can say get pods-o wide.

425
00:32:28,413 --> 00:32:33,230
You can see the pods have
a different IP address than that,

426
00:32:33,230 --> 00:32:35,699
but the service does know it.

427
00:32:35,699 --> 00:32:40,064
So how do we learn more
information about the service?

428
00:32:40,064 --> 00:32:45,762
That's right,
the describe kubectl describe.

429
00:32:45,762 --> 00:32:50,657
So we saw that with the pod, but this time

430
00:32:50,657 --> 00:32:55,704
we're going to describe mynginx service.

431
00:32:55,704 --> 00:33:01,743
You're going to actually have
to specify the type or the kind.

432
00:33:01,743 --> 00:33:06,142
So kubectl, describe service,
mynginx service.

433
00:33:06,142 --> 00:33:11,315
And so what's interesting to note is
these things right here, the endpoints.

434
00:33:11,315 --> 00:33:14,929
So it found the selector
through the selector and

435
00:33:14,929 --> 00:33:19,778
through the labels of these nodes,
the IP address of each of these

436
00:33:19,778 --> 00:33:24,471
pods that are running, and
it keeps it in that set, all right?

437
00:33:24,471 --> 00:33:32,975
So now if we were to do port forwarding,

438
00:33:32,975 --> 00:33:37,097
I would be able to,

439
00:33:37,097 --> 00:33:43,284
instead of saying mynginx,

440
00:33:43,284 --> 00:33:51,299
say mynginx-service, all right?

441
00:33:51,299 --> 00:33:54,161
And now it would forward to any of them.

442
00:33:54,161 --> 00:33:57,449
So now I can say curl

443
00:33:57,449 --> 00:34:04,651
1270.0.0.1000, right?

444
00:34:04,651 --> 00:34:10,913
And what this is doing, which we don't
have it going between the different ones,

445
00:34:10,913 --> 00:34:16,376
but it's actually load balancing
between the three pods that we have.

446
00:34:16,376 --> 00:34:19,475
So I did curl, it used port forwarding,

447
00:34:19,475 --> 00:34:24,501
which then forwarded it to this
IP address inside of the cluster.

448
00:34:24,501 --> 00:34:27,260
And then the first request
went to the pod one.

449
00:34:27,260 --> 00:34:33,366
Second went to this pod,
third one went to the last pod, all right?

450
00:34:33,366 --> 00:34:35,272
So another way you could test that.

451
00:34:35,272 --> 00:34:39,186
We don't have that available
right this second,

452
00:34:39,186 --> 00:34:43,563
but you can write some Python
code to create a service and

453
00:34:43,563 --> 00:34:47,120
just have a different response for
each one.

454
00:34:47,120 --> 00:34:52,138
Okay, so
there's to explore with Kubernetes, but

455
00:34:52,138 --> 00:34:58,083
this should be enough really for
everything we're going to do.

456
00:34:58,083 --> 00:35:02,323
But certainly go try some of
these on your own, all right?

457
00:35:02,323 --> 00:35:03,842
And with that, we'll end here.

458
00:35:03,842 --> 00:35:04,581
Thanks.